+# HUD Kit export plan
+
+Source: `design/figma/hud-kit.json`, the Figma frame **HUD Kit** (`30:1`) in *Block Builder - UI*
+(version `2208815566419836712`, last modified 2026-11-02).
+
+This plan covers every visual on that frame. For each one it says whether we **build** it from
+Roblox UI objects or **export** it from Figma as an image. For each image it gives the export size,
+the sprite sheet it goes on (if any), and the ImageLabel setup in Roblox.
+
+Layers named **Note** are the designer's notes. They are never exported. Hide them before any
+export where a Note is inside the exported frame (this matters for **Panel / Ornate**, which has its
+Note inside it).
+
+The dark navy fill on the HUD Kit frame itself is just the page background. It is not a visual.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Rules used throughout
+
+### Sizes: author at 1x, export at 2x
+
+All UI is authored in offsets for a 1080 px tall screen. `Screen.uiScale()`
+(`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/UI/Screen.luau`) puts a `UIScale` on every ScreenGui that
+grows the UI by `min(viewport) / 1080`. It never goes below 1 and never above **2** (a 4K screen).
+
+So the largest a 32 px icon is ever drawn is 64 real pixels. **Export images at 2x their Figma
+size.** They stay sharp on 4K and are downsampled cleanly on 1080p and phones.
+
+In Roblox, every ImageLabel `Size` uses the **Figma (1x) size in offset pixels**, never the image's
+pixel size. `UIScale` handles the rest.
+
+Exceptions (explained where they come up):
+
+- The **Logo** at 2x is wider than Roblox's 1024 px upload limit, so it is exported at 1024 px wide.
+- **Glow** is blurry anyway, so 1x is enough.
+- **Slot / Empty** is exported at 2x of its *largest* in-game size (128), not its Figma size (96).
+
+### Roblox upload limit
+
+Roblox scales down any uploaded image wider or taller than **1024 px**. Every image and sheet below
+is at most 1024 x 1024.
+
+### When to use a sprite sheet
+
+A sheet only pays off when its pieces are **on screen at the same time**. Then one download covers
+all of them. Pieces that are shown in different situations get their own sheet or their own image,
+so the game doesn't download art the player can't see. Controller prompts are an example: the
+designer notes say each controller's set only shows for players using that controller, so Xbox and
+PlayStation each get their own sheet.
+
+### Sheet padding
+
+Each sheet cell has a **4 px transparent border** (at 2x) around its piece. When Roblox draws part of
+a sheet at a scale that isn't exactly 1:1, it blends in pixels from just outside the rectangle you
+asked for. Without the border, the neighbouring piece shows up as a thin line on the edge.
+
+`ImageRectOffset` points at the piece, **not** at the cell corner. That means cell origin + 4.
+
+### How to build a sheet in Figma
+
+1. Make a new frame at **half** the sheet size (the 1x size), with no fill and Clip content on.
+2. Paste a 1x copy of each piece at its **1x** position, which is half of the 2x coordinates in the
+   tables below. All 1x positions are whole numbers.
+3. Apply the per-sheet changes listed below (for example, recolour the icons white).
+4. Export the frame as PNG at **2x**. The result is exactly the sheet size in the table.
+
+### ImageLabel defaults
+
+Unless a section says otherwise, every exported image is drawn with:
+
+| Property | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `BackgroundTransparency` | `1` |
+| `ScaleType` | `Enum.ScaleType.Stretch` |
+| `ResampleMode` | `Enum.ResamplerMode.Default` |
+| `ImageColor3` | `Color3.new(1, 1, 1)` |
+| `ImageTransparency` | `0` |
+| `Size` | `UDim2.fromOffset(<Figma width>, <Figma height>)` |
+
