+### 3.5 Panel / Ornate → `PanelOrnate` (9-slice)
+
+Figma: frame `40:137`, 480 x 320. Dark brown fill, 4 px gold inside stroke, four 48 x 48 gold corner
+flourishes (`40:84` to `40:87`). Designer note: *"Dialog background. Stretches to fit its content,
+from 240 x 160 up to 960 x 640. Corner flourishes must not stretch."*
+
+Stretches but has fixed corners, so this is a **9-slice**. We don't export the panel at 480 x 320.
+Export a **shrunk copy** that only has the corners plus a small stretchable middle:
+
+1. Duplicate the frame. **Delete or hide the Note** (`40:136`). It sits inside the panel.
+2. Resize the copy to **112 x 112** (48 corner + 16 middle + 48 corner) with the corners pinned to
+   their corners. The fill and the 4 px stroke stay as they are.
+3. Export at 2x → **224 x 224**.
+
+The middle strip (pixels 96 to 128 at 2x) is plain fill on the inside and plain fill + stroke on the
+edges. It stretches without visible distortion.
+
+ImageLabel (on top of the defaults):
+
+| Property | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `Image` | `PanelOrnate` asset id |
+| `ScaleType` | `Enum.ScaleType.Slice` |
+| `SliceCenter` | `Rect.new(96, 96, 128, 128)` |
+| `SliceScale` | `0.5` |
+| `Size` | the dialog's size, 240 x 160 to 960 x 640 offset |
+
+`SliceScale = 0.5` makes the 96 px image corners draw at 48 authored px, which matches Figma.
+`UIScale` then scales them back up to 96 real px at 4K. The smallest allowed panel (240 x 160) is
+still much larger than the two corners added together (96), so the corners never overlap.
+
+### 3.6 Slot / Empty → `SlotEmpty`
+
+Figma: frame `40:139`, 96 x 96, corner radius 12, **dashed** 2 px stroke (8 on / 6 off), white at
+30%, no fill. Designer note: *"Empty inventory slot. Slot size is a player setting from 80 to 128."*
+
+- `UIStroke` can't draw dashes, so this has to be an image.
+- A 9-slice would stretch the dashes unevenly along the edges. The slot is always square, so we use
+  **one image scaled evenly**.
+- Export for the **largest** setting: 128 at 2x = **256 x 256**. In Figma, export the 96 x 96 frame
+  with the size set to **256w**. That scales the whole thing, stroke and dashes included, so it
+  matches the design at every slot size.
+- In the copy you export, set the stroke to **100% opacity white**. The 30% opacity is applied in
+  Roblox, so code can brighten the slot on hover or drag-over without a second image.
+
+ImageLabel (on top of the defaults):
+
+| Property | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `Image` | `SlotEmpty` asset id |
+| `Size` | `UDim2.fromOffset(slotSize, slotSize)` (80 to 128) |
+| `ImageTransparency` | `0.7` (Figma's 30% opacity) |
+
+Design check: because the image scales evenly, the stroke draws at about 1.7 authored px at size 80
+and about 2.7 px at size 128 (2 px at 96). If the designer needs exactly 2 px at every size, ask
+them first. It would take one export per size setting.
+
+### 3.7 Background / Dot Grid → `DotGridTile` (tiled)
+
+Figma: frame `40:142` (a 192 x 96 sample) at 5% opacity. It has 2 px white dots every 24 px, each
+dot's centre 12 px in from its tile's corner. Designer note (`40:143`): *"The dot grid repeats
+behind every full-screen menu, at any screen size."*
+
+It repeats at any size, so export **one tile** and let Roblox repeat it:
+
+1. Make a new 24 x 24 frame with no fill.
+2. Put one 2 x 2 white dot at x = 11, y = 11 (centred) at **100% opacity**. Don't copy the sample
+   frame's 5% opacity.
+3. Export at 2x → **48 x 48** (4 x 4 dot centred).
+
+The 5% opacity goes in Roblox, not in the PNG. Baked into the PNG, 5% alpha rounds to about 13/255
+and you get visible banding.
+
+ImageLabel (on top of the defaults):
+
+| Property | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `Image` | `DotGridTile` asset id |
+| `ScaleType` | `Enum.ScaleType.Tile` |
+| `TileSize` | `UDim2.fromOffset(24, 24)` |
+| `ImageTransparency` | `0.95` |
+| `Size` | `UDim2.fromScale(1, 1)` (fills the menu) |
+
